# Is a QALY a QALY, whoever gets it?
prompt: "What is the ethical case for counting every QALY equally? Do QALYs discriminate against the old and the disabled? Should severity, fair innings or the rule of rescue add weight — and how do payers such as NICE and Norway handle this?"

## A QALY is a QALY?
use: qaly_profiles
with:
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 150
      w: 690
      h: 510
    lines: straight
    x_label: Years from age 60
    y_label: Quality, q
    x_max: 6
    profiles:
      - {id: std, label: 'Standard: 2.0', color: '#2f6b8f', waypoints: [{t: 0, u: 0.5}, {t: 4, u: 0.5}], death_at: 4}
      - {id: new, label: 'Treatment: 3.5', color: '#b5482e', waypoints: [{t: 0, u: 0.7}, {t: 5, u: 0.7}], death_at: 5}
    shade_between:
      a: new
      b: std
      gain_label: ''
      loss_label: ''

    box claim_a "Just arithmetic" in claims hidden true
    box claim_b "A moral choice" in claims hidden true
    column claims gap 50 fit.h 240 fit.w 250 fit.x 730 fit.y 340 hidden true
    mark strike_a around claim_a kind strike hidden true
    text icer "ICER 20,000 = λ" #8a5fa8 x 850 y 240 font_size 26 hidden true
    text dq "ΔQ = 1.5" #4f7a43 at.data [1.6,0.6] font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "A QALY is a QALY?"

Some say a QALY is just neutral arithmetic, the same for everyone; others say it hides a moral choice about who counts.
    draw axes full_health_line tick_one tick_zero claim_a claim_b

Who is right?
    highlight claim_a claim_b

    pause 0.5

Take our 60-year-old: standard care gives four years at a quality of 0.5, which is two QALYs.
    draw curve_std label_std

The new treatment gives five years at 0.7, three and a half QALYs, and the gain between them is 1.5.
    draw curve_new label_new gain_regions dq

    pause 0.3

The sum asks how much health is gained, never whose — a QALY to this patient weighs exactly what it would to anyone else.
    focus gain_regions dq curve_new curve_std

That is the first case for equal weighting: impartiality, no one's health counts for more because of who they are.
    highlight dq effect circle

The second is the budget: the extra 30,000 spent here, at a threshold of 20,000 per QALY, would buy 1.5 QALYs for someone else.
    draw icer

So any extra weight for one group is health taken from patients we never see.
    highlight icer

Yet the sum did choose something: it gave every person's QALYs a weight of one.
    text weight "× 1" #3d3833 at.data [4.5,0.3] font_size 30

That is a moral choice too, so 'just arithmetic' is wrong.
    draw strike_a

Equal weighting is a defensible choice, not the absence of one.
    highlight claim_b effect circle

    erase claims strike_a icer

And equal per QALY is not equal per person — which is where the critics begin.
    highlight label_std label_new

## The old and the disabled
use: qaly_profiles
with:
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 150
      w: 740
      h: 510
    lines: straight
    x_max: 95
    y_label: Quality, q
    shade_between: null
    profiles:
      - {id: young, label: 'Age 30: 40', color: '#2f6b8f', fill: true, waypoints: [{t: 30, u: 0.8}, {t: 80, u: 0.8}], death_at: 80}
      - {id: old, label: 'Age 70: 8', color: '#b5482e', fill: true, waypoints: [{t: 70, u: 0.8}, {t: 80, u: 0.8}], death_at: 80}
      - {id: dis, label: 'Disabled: 20', color: '#8a5fa8', fill: true, waypoints: [{t: 30, u: 0.4}, {t: 80, u: 0.4}], death_at: 80}
    x_label: Age
sources:
    - id: harris
      title: QALYfying the value of life
      authors: John Harris
      year: 1987
      finding: QALYs discriminate against the old and the disabled

    box harris_card "Harris, 1987" cites ["harris"] x 870 y 560 hidden true
    text harris_cue "Each life, equal" below harris_card gap 18 #3d3833 font_size 24 hidden true
    text t30 "30" #8f887c at.data [30,-0.06] font_size 22 hidden true
    text t70 "70" #8f887c at.data [70,-0.06] font_size 22 hidden true
    text t80 "80" #8f887c at.data [80,-0.06] font_size 22 hidden true

    card title "The old and the disabled"

Now that we have seen the case for weighting every QALY equally, look at who ends up with fewer QALYs to gain.
    draw axes full_health_line tick_one tick_zero t30 t70 t80

Say a cure saves a life, and afterwards the person lives to 80 at a quality of 0.8 — illustrative numbers.
    draw curve_young

Saved at 30, that is fifty years at 0.8: forty QALYs.
    draw fill_young label_young

    fade fill_young curve_young label_young to 0.3

Saved at 70, the same cure buys ten years: eight QALYs.
    draw curve_old fill_old label_old

Same treatment, same cost, but the older patient's rescue counts for a fifth as much.
    highlight label_old effect circle

    erase curve_old fill_old label_old

Now a young patient with a lasting disability, living at 0.4 after the cure.
    draw curve_dis

The same fifty years give only twenty QALYs: half the priority, because of the disability.
    draw fill_dis label_dis

In 1987 the philosopher John Harris attacked exactly this, in a paper called 'QALYfying the value of life'.
    draw harris_card harris_cue

His point: each person who wants to go on living has the same claim to it, however many QALYs their life holds.
    highlight harris_cue

Critics call the disability case double jeopardy: the first misfortune lowers your quality of life, and then it lowers your priority.
    highlight curve_dis

Defenders reply that the QALY counts health gained, not the worth of a person, and that ignoring it means buying less health overall.
    focus fill_dis curve_dis label_dis fill_young curve_young

    erase harris_card harris_cue

So QALYs do not rank people, but wherever there is less health to gain, the old and the disabled come second.
    draw curve_old fill_old label_old

## Fair innings and rescue
use: qaly_profiles
with:
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 150
      w: 700
      h: 510
    lines: straight
    x_max: 90
    x_label: Age
    y_label: Quality, q
    shade_between: null
    reference:
      label: Fair innings
      waypoints: [{t: 0, u: 0.92}, {t: 40, u: 0.88}, {t: 82, u: 0.75}]
      death_at: 82
    profiles:
      - {id: early, label: Struck at 30, color: '#b5482e', waypoints: [{t: 0, u: 0.92}, {t: 30, u: 0.89}, {t: 30, u: 0.5, step: true}, {t: 40, u: 0.45}], death_at: 40}
      - {id: sixty, label: Our 60-year-old, color: '#2f6b8f', waypoints: [{t: 0, u: 0.92}, {t: 60, u: 0.8}, {t: 60, u: 0.5, step: true}, {t: 64, u: 0.5}], death_at: 64}
    shortfall:
      - {of: early, index_age: 30, areas: true}
      - {of: sixty, index_age: 60, areas: true}
sources:
    - id: williams
      title: 'Intergenerational equity: an exploration of the ''fair innings'' argument'
      authors: Alan Williams
      year: 1997
      finding: extra weight for those who would fall short of a fair innings

    population crowd fit.h 300 fit.w 230 fit.x 745 fit.y 300 colors.seen #b5482e colors.unseen #8f887c count 13 states.seen 1 states.unseen 12 order rows labels.seen Seen labels.unseen Unseen hidden true
    text t30 "30" #8f887c at.data [30,-0.06] font_size 22 hidden true
    text t60 "60" #8f887c at.data [60,-0.06] font_size 22 hidden true
    text t82 "82" #8f887c at.data [82,-0.06] font_size 22 hidden true

    card title "Fair innings and rescue"

    hide shortfall_note shortfall_note_2 index_line index_line_2 label_index label_index_2 shortfall_region shortfall_region_2 expected_region expected_region_2 kept_region kept_region_2

Now that we have seen the complaint that QALYs favour the young, here is an argument that they should favour them even more.
    draw axes full_health_line tick_one tick_zero t30 t60 t82

Alan Williams, one of the QALY's pioneers, made it in 1997: the fair innings argument.
    draw curve_sixty label_sixty curve_early label_early

Everyone, he argued, has a claim to a fair lifetime of health — a normal span in reasonable quality.
    draw reference_curve label_reference

The dashed curve is such a life, lived to its natural end; the area beneath it is a fair innings.
    highlight reference_curve

Our 60-year-old has already had most of one; someone struck down at 30 falls far short.
    draw lifetime_region_2 label_lifetime_2 lifetime_region label_lifetime

Williams proposed extra weight for QALYs that go to those who would otherwise fall short of a fair innings.
    highlight label_lifetime effect circle

On this view the young patient's QALYs count for more — the opposite of Harris's remedy.
    focus lifetime_region label_lifetime curve_early reference_curve

The rule of rescue pulls another way: the urge to save an identified person in danger, whatever it costs.
    draw crowd_seen

One named patient in the news, against many unnamed patients who would gain more from the same money.
    draw crowd crowd_legend

The QALY sum cannot see the difference: a named life and a statistical one weigh the same.
    highlight crowd

Defenders call that fairness to the unseen; critics call it blindness to something people really value.
    highlight crowd_unseen

    erase crowd crowd_legend

So both arguments ask the same of the sum: add weight — for a lifetime cut short, or for a person we can see.
    highlight label_lifetime reference_curve

## Severity, in practice
use: qaly_profiles
with:
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 150
      w: 640
      h: 510
    lines: straight
    x_max: 23
    x_label: Years from age 60
    y_label: Quality, q
    index_age: 0
    reference:
      label: Normal life
      waypoints: [{t: 0, u: 0.86}, {t: 21, u: 0.84}]
      death_at: 21
    profiles:
      - {id: std, label: Standard, color: '#2f6b8f', waypoints: [{t: 0, u: 0.5}, {t: 4, u: 0.5}], death_at: 4}
      - {id: new, label: Treatment, color: '#b5482e', waypoints: [{t: 0, u: 0.7}, {t: 5, u: 0.7}], death_at: 5}
    shade_between:
      a: new
      b: std
      gain_label: ΔQ = 1.5
      loss_label: ''
    shortfall:
      of: std
      show: both
      label: Health lost

    scratch calc top-right work ["NICE 2022: × 1.2 or × 1.7",{"tex":"1.5 \\times 1.2 = 1.8"},{"tex":"30{,}000 \\div 1.8 \\approx 16{,}700"}] hidden true
    text l_exp "Expected: 17.8" #8f887c x 820 y 470 font_size 26 hidden true
    text l_left "Left: 2.0" #2f6b8f x 820 y 420 font_size 26 hidden true
    text l_lost "Lost: 15.8" #3d3833 x 820 y 370 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Severity, in practice"

    hide shortfall_note index_line label_index

Now that we have seen the arguments for extra weight, the one payers have actually adopted is severity.
    draw axes full_health_line tick_one tick_zero curve_std label_std curve_new label_new gain_regions label_gain

Severity asks how much health the disease takes: the gap between a normal life from 60 and what this patient can expect.
    draw reference_curve label_reference

With illustrative population norms, our 60-year-old could expect about eighteen more QALYs; standard care leaves two.
    draw l_exp l_left

That gap, about sixteen QALYs, is the absolute shortfall — the measure of severity Norway uses.
    draw shortfall_region l_lost

Norway's priority-setting weighs three things: the benefit, the resources it takes, and the severity.
    focus shortfall_region reference_curve curve_std l_lost

The more severe the condition, the higher the cost per QALY Norway will accept.
    highlight l_lost

In 2022 NICE, in England, brought in a severity modifier: QALYs in the most severe conditions count 1.2 or 1.7 times.
    draw calc_box calc_line_1

Our patient loses about 89 percent of the health they could expect: enough for the 1.2 weight.
    text l_prop "= 89% of it" #3d3833 x 820 y 325 font_size 26

Then the gain of 1.5 QALYs counts as 1.8, and the ICER falls from 20,000 to about 16,700 per QALY.
    draw calc_line_2 calc_line_3

    erase calc

The United States went another way: the Affordable Care Act restricts the use of cost per QALY thresholds in Medicare.
    text us "US Medicare: no λ" #8a5fa8 x 820 y 230 font_size 26

So a QALY is a QALY only because someone decided it should be; every weight, even a weight of one, is a choice about whose health the budget buys.
    focus gain_regions label_gain shortfall_region

